     1                                  ; Itsy Forth
     2                                  ;    Written by John Metcalf
     3                                  ;    Commentary by John Metcalf and Mike Adams
     4                                  ;
     5                                  ; Itsy Forth was written for use with NASM, the "Netwide Assembler"
     6                                  ; that's available for free download (http://www.nasm.us/).
     7                                  ; The command line for assembling Itsy is:
     8                                  ;
     9                                  ;      nasm itsy.asm -fbin -o itsy.com
    10                                  ;
    11                                  ; If you wish to have an assembly listing, give it this command:
    12                                  ;
    13                                  ;      nasm itsy.asm -fbin -l itsy.lst -o itsy.com
    14                                  ;
    15                                  ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    16                                  ; Implementation notes:
    17                                  ;
    18                                  ; Register Usage:
    19                                  ;    sp - data stack pointer.
    20                                  ;    bp - return stack pointer.
    21                                  ;    si - Forth instruction pointer.
    22                                  ;    di - pointer to current XT (CFA of word currently being executed).
    23                                  ;    bx - TOS (top of data stack). The top value on the data stack is not
    24                                  ;         actually kept on the CPU's data stack. It's kept in the BX register.
    25                                  ;         Having it in a register like this speeds up the operation of
    26                                  ;         the primitive words. They don't have to take the time to pull a
    27                                  ;         value off of the stack; it's already in a register where it can
    28                                  ;         be used right away!
    29                                  ;    ax, cd, dx - Can all be freely used for processing data. The other
    30                                  ;         registers can still be used also, but only with caution. Their
    31                                  ;         contents must be pushed to the stack and then restored before
    32                                  ;         exiting from the word or calling any other Forth words. LOTS of
    33                                  ;         potential for program crashes if you don't do this correctly.
    34                                  ;         The notable exception is the DI register, which can (and is, below)
    35                                  ;         used pretty freely in assembly code, since the concept of a pointer
    36                                  ;         to the current CFA is rather irrelevant in assembly.
    37                                  ;
    38                                  ;
    39                                  ; Structure of an Itsy word definition:
    40                                  ;     # of
    41                                  ;    Bytes:   Description:
    42                                  ;    ------   ---------------------------------------------------------
    43                                  ;      2      Link Field. Contains the address of the link field of the
    44                                  ;                definition preceding this one in the dictionary. The link
    45                                  ;                field of the first def in the dictionary contains 0.
    46                                  ;    Varies   Name Field. The first byte of the name field contains the length
    47                                  ;                of the name; succeeding bytes contain the ASCII characters of
    48                                  ;                the name itself. If the high bit of the length is set, the
    49                                  ;                definition is tagged as being an "immediate" word.
    50                                  ;      2      Code Field. Contains the address of the executable code for
    51                                  ;                the word. For primitives, this will likely be the address
    52                                  ;                of the word's own data field. Note that the header creation
    53                                  ;                macros automatically generate labels for the code field
    54                                  ;                addresses of the words they're used to define, though the
    55                                  ;                CFA labels aren't visible in the code shown below. The
    56                                  ;                assembler macros create labels, known as "execution tags"
    57                                  ;                or XTs, for the code field of each word.
    58                                  ;    Varies   Data Field. Contains either a list of the code field addresses
    59                                  ;                of the words that make up this definition, or assembly-
    60                                  ;                language code for primitives, or numeric data for variables
    61                                  ;                 and constants and such.
    62                                  
    63                                  
    64                                  ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    65                                  ;
    66                                  ; Beginning of actual code.
    67                                  ;
    68                                  ; Include the definitions of the macros that are used in NASM to create
    69                                  ; the headers of the words. See macros.asm for more details.
    70                                  ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    71                                  %include "macros.asm"
     1                              <1> ; Itsy Forth - Macros
     2                              <1> ;   Written by John Metcalf
     3                              <1> ;   Commentary by Mike Adams
     4                              <1> ;
     5                              <1> ; Itsy Forth was written for use with NASM, the "Netwide Assembler"
     6                              <1> ; (http://www.nasm.us/). It uses a number of macros to deal with the tedium
     7                              <1> ; of generating the headers for the words that are defined in Itsy's source
     8                              <1> ; code file. The macros, and the explanations of what they're doing, are
     9                              <1> ; listed below:
    10                              <1> 
    11                              <1> ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    12                              <1> ; First, two variables are defined for use by the macros:
    13                              <1>         ; link is the initial value for the first link field that'll
    14                              <1>         ; be defined. It's value will be updated with each header
    15                              <1>         ; that's created.
    16                              <1>         %define link 0
    17                              <1> 
    18                              <1>         ; A bitmask that'll be called "immediate" will be used to
    19                              <1>         ; encode the flag into the length bytes of word names in order
    20                              <1>         ; to indicate that the word will be of the immediate type.
    21                              <1>         %define immediate 080h
    22                              <1> 
    23                              <1> ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    24                              <1> ; The first macro defined is the primary one used by the others, "head".
    25                              <1> ; It does the lion's share of the work for the other macros that'll be
    26                              <1> ; defined afterwards. Its commands perform the following operations:
    27                              <1> 
    28                              <1>         ; The first line of the macro declares it's name as "head".
    29                              <1>         ; The 4 in this line signifies that it expects to receive
    30                              <1>         ; 4 parameters when it's invoked: the string that will be the
    31                              <1>         ; word's name and will be encoded into the header along with
    32                              <1>         ; the string's name; an "execution tag" name that will have the
    33                              <1>         ; prefix "xt_" attached to it and will be used as a label for
    34                              <1>         ; the word's code field; a flag that will be 080h if the word
    35                              <1>         ; will be immediate and a 0 otherwise; and the label for the
    36                              <1>         ; word's runtime code, whose address will be put into the
    37                              <1>         ; word's code field.
    38                              <1>         %macro head 4
    39                              <1> 
    40                              <1>         ; Okay, what we're doing in this odd-looking bit of code is
    41                              <1>         ; declaring a variable called "%%link" that's local only to this
    42                              <1>         ; macro and is independent of the earlier variable we declared
    43                              <1>         ; as "link". It's a label that will represent the current
    44                              <1>         ; location in the object code we're creating. Then we lay down
    45                              <1>         ; some actual object code, using the "dw" command to write the
    46                              <1>         ; current value of "link" into the executable file.
    47                              <1>         %%link dw link
    48                              <1> 
    49                              <1>         ; Here's one of the tricky parts. We now redefine the value of
    50                              <1>         ; "link" to be whatever the current value of "%%link" is, which
    51                              <1>         ; is basically the address of the link field that was created
    52                              <1>         ; during this particular use of this macro. That way, the next
    53                              <1>         ; time head is called, the value that will be written into the
    54                              <1>         ; code in the "dw" command above will be whatever the value of
    55                              <1>         ; "%%link" was during THIS use of the macro. This way, each time
    56                              <1>         ; head is called, the value that'll be written into the new
    57                              <1>         ; link field will be the address that was used for the link
    58                              <1>         ; field the previous time head was called, which is just how
    59                              <1>         ; we want the link fields to be in a Forth dictionary. Note that
    60                              <1>         ; the first time that head is called, the value of link was
    61                              <1>         ; predefined as 0, so that the link field of the first word in
    62                              <1>         ; the dictionary will contain the value of 0 to mark it as
    63                              <1>         ; being the first word in the dictionary.
    64                              <1>         %define link %%link
    65                              <1> 
    66                              <1>         ; Now the name field. The first argument passed to head is the
    67                              <1>         ; string defining the new word's name. The next line in the macro
    68                              <1>         ; measures the length of the string (the "%1" tells it that it's
    69                              <1>         ; supposed to look at argument #1) and assigns it to a macro-local
    70                              <1>         ; variable called "%%count".
    71                              <1>         %strlen %%count %1
    72                              <1> 
    73                              <1>         ; In this next line, we're writing data into the object code on
    74                              <1>         ; a byte-by-byte basis. We first write a byte consisting of the
    75                              <1>         ; value of argument 3 (which is 080h if we're writing the header
    76                              <1>         ; for an immediate word or a 0 otherwise) added to the length of
    77                              <1>         ; the name string to produce the length byte in the header. Then
    78                              <1>         ; we write the name string itself into the file.
    79                              <1>         db %3 + %%count,%1
    80                              <1> 
    81                              <1>         ; Okay, don't get confused by the "+" in this next line. Take
    82                              <1>         ; careful note of the spaces; the actual command is "%+", which
    83                              <1>         ; is string concatenation, not numeric addition. We're going to
    84                              <1>         ; splice a string together. The first part consists of the "xt_",
    85                              <1>         ; then we splice the macro's 2nd argument onto it. The resulting
    86                              <1>         ; string is used as the head's "execution tag", the address of
    87                              <1>         ; it's code field. This label is then used for the "dw" command
    88                              <1>         ; that writes the value of argument #4 (the address of the word's
    89                              <1>         ; runtime code) into the header's code field.
    90                              <1>         xt_ %+ %2 dw %4
    91                              <1> 
    92                              <1>         ; As you might guess, the next line marks the end of the
    93                              <1>         ; macro's definition. The entire header's been defined at this
    94                              <1>         ; point, and we're now ready for the data field, whether it's
    95                              <1>         ; composed of assembly code, a list of Forth words, or the
    96                              <1>         ; numeric data for a variable or constant.
    97                              <1>         %endmacro
    98                              <1> 
    99                              <1> ; For example, calling head with the following line:
   100                              <1> ;
   101                              <1> ;      head,'does>',does,080h,docolon
   102                              <1> ;
   103                              <1> ; will produce the following header code...
   104                              <1> ;
   105                              <1> ;               dw (address of link of previous header)
   106                              <1> ;               db 085h,'does>'
   107                              <1> ;      xt_does  dw docolon
   108                              <1> ;
   109                              <1> ; ...and records the address of this header's link field so that it can
   110                              <1> ; be written into the link field of the next word, just as the address
   111                              <1> ; of the previous link field was written into this header.
   112                              <1> ; This method saves the programmer a lot of tedium in manually generating
   113                              <1> ; the code for word headers when writing a Forth system's kernel in
   114                              <1> ; assembly language. Note that argument #2 is surrounded by single quotes.
   115                              <1> ; That's the format that the assembler expects to see when being told to
   116                              <1> ; lay down a string of characters byte-by-byte in a db command, so they
   117                              <1> ; have to be present when they're given as an arg to this macro so that
   118                              <1> ; the macro puts them in their proper place.
   119                              <1> 
   120                              <1> ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
   121                              <1> ; The next macro is called "primitive", and is used for setting up a header
   122                              <1> ; for a word written in assembly language.
   123                              <1> ;
   124                              <1>         ; Here we declare the definition of the macro called "primitive".
   125                              <1>         ; Note, though, the odd manner in which the number of required
   126                              <1>         ; arguments is stated. Yes, that really does mean that it can
   127                              <1>         ; take from 2 to 3 arguments. Well, what does it do if the user
   128                              <1>         ; only gives it 2? That's what that 0 is: the default value that's
   129                              <1>         ; to be used for argument #3 if the user doesn't specify it. Most
   130                              <1>         ; of the time he won't; the only time arg #3 will be specifically
   131                              <1>         ; given will be if the user is defining an immediate word.
   132                              <1>         %macro primitive 2-3 0
   133                              <1> 
   134                              <1>         ; All primitive does is to pass its arguments on to head, which
   135                              <1>         ; does most of the actual work. It passes on the word name and
   136                              <1>         ; the execution tag name as-is. Parameter #3 will be given the
   137                              <1>         ; default value of 0 unless the user specifically states it.
   138                              <1>         ; This is meant to allow the user to add "immediate" to the
   139                              <1>         ; macro invocation to create an immediate word. The 4th arg,
   140                              <1>         ; "$+2", means that when head goes to write the address of the
   141                              <1>         ; run-time code into the code field, the address it's going to
   142                              <1>         ; use will be 2 bytes further along than the code field address,
   143                              <1>         ; i.e. the address of the start of the code immediately after
   144                              <1>         ; the code field. (The "$" symbol is used by most assemblers
   145                              <1>         ; to represent the address of the code that's currently being
   146                              <1>         ; assembled.)
   147                              <1>         head %1,%2,%3,$+2
   148                              <1> 
   149                              <1>         ; End of the macro definition.
   150                              <1>         %endmacro
   151                              <1> 
   152                              <1> ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
   153                              <1> ; The macro "colon" operates very similarly to "primitive", except that
   154                              <1> ; it's used for colon definitions:
   155                              <1> ;
   156                              <1>         ; Declare the macro, with 2 to 3 arguments, using 0 for the default
   157                              <1>         ; value of arg #3 if one isn't specifically given.
   158                              <1>         %macro colon 2-3 0
   159                              <1> 
   160                              <1>         ; Pass the args on to head, using docolon as the runtime code.
   161                              <1>         head %1,%2,%3,docolon
   162                              <1> 
   163                              <1>         ; End of macro definition.
   164                              <1>         %endmacro
   165                              <1> 
   166                              <1> ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
   167                              <1> ; The rest of the macros all require a specific number of arguments, since
   168                              <1> ; none of them have the option of being immediate. This one defines
   169                              <1> ; a constant:
   170                              <1> 
   171                              <1>         ; Macro name is, unsurprisingly, "constant", and gets 3 arguments.
   172                              <1>         ; As with head and primitive, the first 2 are the word's name and
   173                              <1>         ; the label name that'll be used for the word. The third argument
   174                              <1>         ; is the value that we want the constant to hold.
   175                              <1>         %macro constant 3
   176                              <1> 
   177                              <1>         ; Use the head macro. Args 1 and 2, the names, get passed on as-is.
   178                              <1>         ; Constants are never defined as immediate (though it's an intriguing
   179                              <1>         ; idea; a constant whose value is one thing when compiling and
   180                              <1>         ; another when interpreting might be useful for something), so arg #3
   181                              <1>         ; passed on to head is always a 0, and arg #4 will always be doconst,
   182                              <1>         ; the address of the runtime code for constants.
   183                              <1>         head %1,%2,0,doconst
   184                              <1> 
   185                              <1>         ; Similar to the way that the label is created for the execution
   186                              <1>         ; tags, here we create a label for the data field of the constant,
   187                              <1>         ; though this time we're prefixing the name with "val_" instead
   188                              <1>         ; of the "xt_" used for the execution tags. Then we use a dw to
   189                              <1>         ; write constant's arg #3, the constant's value, into the code.
   190                              <1>         val_ %+ %2 dw %3
   191                              <1> 
   192                              <1>         ; End of the definition.
   193                              <1>         %endmacro
   194                              <1> 
   195                              <1> ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
   196                              <1> ; The macro for variables is very similar to the one for constants.
   197                              <1> 
   198                              <1>         ; Macro name "variable", 3 arguments, with arg #3 being the
   199                              <1>         ; initial value that will be given to the variable.
   200                              <1>         %macro variable 3
   201                              <1> 
   202                              <1>         ; Just like in "constant", except that the runtime code is dovar.
   203                              <1>         head %1,%2,0,dovar
   204                              <1> 
   205                              <1>         ; Exact same line as used in "constant", with the same effects.
   206                              <1>         val_ %+ %2 dw %3
   207                              <1> 
   208                              <1>         ; End of the definition.
   209                              <1>         %endmacro
   210                              <1> 
   211                              <1> ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
   212                              <1> ;
   213                              <1> ; That's the last of the macros. They're accessed through the
   214                              <1> ; "%include macros.asm" command near the beginning of Itsy's
   215                              <1> ; source code file. Or, if you prefer, you can remove the
   216                              <1> ; %include command and splice the above code directly
   217                              <1> ; into itsy.asm in its place.
   218                              <1> ;
   219                              <1> ;--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    72                                  
    73                                  ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    74                                  ; Define the location for the stack. -256 decimal = 0ff00h
    75                                  ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    76                                  stack0  equ -256
    77                                  
    78                                  ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    79                                  ; Set the starting point for the executable code. 0100h is the standard
    80                                  ; origin for programs running under MS-DOS or its equivalents.
    81                                  ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    82                                          org 0100h
    83                                  
    84                                  ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    85                                  ; Jump to the location of the start of Itsy's initialization code.
    86                                  ;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    87 00000000 EB54                            jmp xt_abort+2
    88                                  
